// AtomicWrite+acquire -- an atomic write is an acquire as well as a release
// (section 9.2). The observer thread fixes co on A to 1 then 2. In OCaml the
// write of 2 happens before the write of b after it, and co on atomics is in
// hb, so r1 = 1 closes a cycle in hb | rf. C++ and RC11 allow it: an sc store
// is only a release, and b := 1 may move before it. This is why OCaml compiles
// an atomic store to xchg on x86, not to a plain store.
//
// The OCaml memory model (Dolan, Sivaramakrishnan & Madhavapeddy, PLDI 2018,
// section 6). An access in mode sc is to an atomic location, any other access
// non-atomic.
A := 0;
b := 0;
{
  r1 := b;
  A.store(1, sc)
} ||| {
  A.store(2, sc);
  b := 1
} ||| {
  r3 := A.load(sc);
  r4 := A.load(sc)
}
%%
forbid (r1 = 1 && r3 = 1 && r4 = 2) [OCaml]
allow (r1 = 1 && r3 = 1 && r4 = 2) [RC11]
allow (r1 = 1 && r3 = 1 && r4 = 2) [smrd]
forbid (r1 = 1 && r3 = 1 && r4 = 2) [SC]
